WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000
Let's take a look at the components that make up a typical hydraulic braking system.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:29.000
We have the brake pedal, the push rod, the servo, the master cylinder,

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:37.000
the reservoir,

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:43.000
and hydraulic lines.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:55.000
The brake pedal transfers the mechanical force supplied by the driver to the push rod.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:03.000
The push rod transfers the mechanical force supplied by the brake pedal through the servo to the master cylinder.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:11.000
The brake servo or vacuum booster assists the driver by applying additional force to the braking system.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:25.000
The master cylinder converts non-hydraulic pressure from the driver's foot on the brake pedal into hydraulic pressure.

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:34.000
When braking, pistons with seals and springs in the master cylinder create hydraulic force which pressurizes the entire hydraulic system.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:40.000
Brake fluid returns to the master cylinder when pressure is released.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:48.000
The reservoir holds sufficient brake fluid to stop air getting into the hydraulic system.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:55.000
Finally, we have the hydraulic lines which carry the brake fluid to the front and rear brakes.

00:01:55.000 --> 00:02:01.000
Now, let's take a look at the hydraulic system in action.

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:08.000
Here we can see the wheels turning.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:20.000
Pressing the brake pedal extends a push rod through the brake servo and into the master cylinder.

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:35.000
The master cylinder pressurizes the entire hydraulic system and forces brake fluid through the hydraulic lines.

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:40.000
Releasing the brake pedal reverses the process.

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:47.000
The hydraulic pressure on the entire brake system is reduced allowing brake fluid to return to the master cylinder.

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:59.000
A combination of seals in the caliper and springs in the brake drum then return the brakes to their original position, allowing the wheels to turn freely once more.

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:12.000
Brake fluid travels through the hydraulic lines, applying pressure to the caliper or wheel cylinder pistons, which in turn create the friction force required to slow the wheels.

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:19.000
And that's the basics of how a hydraulic brake system works.

